  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a single case study of a specific station development in Hong Kong, which may limit their direct applicability to different institutional and planning contexts.",
      "what": "This study analyzes process justice in the planning of the Kai Tak station transit-oriented development (TOD) in Hong Kong. Using the Rules-in-Use lens from the Institutional Analysis and Development (IAD) framework, the research evaluates stakeholder dynamics and interactions during the planning phase. The analysis identifies four systemic issues: government dominance, power imbalances between transit and housing stakeholders, conflicting rules-in-use between internal project teams and external actors, and pervasive ambiguity in planning rules.",
      "why": "It shifts the scholarly focus of transit-oriented development from outcome-based equity to process-based justice, revealing how institutional rules and power imbalances during the planning phase systematically marginalize external stakeholders."
    }
  },
